+
+An `[E; N]` that lives in a buffer is one JavaScript array in one place of that buffer, on the stack
+and on the heap alike, with one exception, bytes, below: `[[E; N]; M]`, a `&[[E; N]]`, a
+`Box<[E; N]>` and a `Vec<[E; N]>` are all one JavaScript array per row, and a pointer to `[E; N]`
+into one is a plain slot, so the place projections (`p.buf[p.off][i]`), the strict offset and the
+element moves read every one of them the same way.
